  "hints": [
    "if you squint, a 'letter' and a 'word' are just two kinds of tokens that need a consistent one-to-one mapping - which other problem does this remind you of?",
    "first split s on spaces, and check the word count matches the pattern length - if not, you're done immediately",
    "keep two maps: letter -> word, and word -> letter. walk through both sequences together, one position at a time",
    "at each position, if either map already has an entry, check it matches; if not, record it in both maps - any mismatch means the pattern doesn't hold"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting to check that the word count matches the pattern length before comparing",
    "only mapping letter to word and not the reverse, missing cases where two letters map to the same word",
    "not splitting on whitespace correctly, or mishandling extra spaces"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a `pattern` string of letters and a string `s` of space-separated words.\n\n**Determine whether `s` follows the same pattern, meaning there's a consistent one-to-one mapping between each letter in `pattern` and each word in `s`, in both directions.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= pattern.length <= 300",
      "1 <= s.length <= 3000",
      "pattern consists of lowercase English letters",
      "s consists of lowercase English letters and single spaces, with no leading or trailing spaces"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "different letter count vs word count (always false)",
      "two different letters mapping to the same word",
      "a single letter and a single word"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
